<div class="container"><div class="lesson-body">
<h2>Extreme Value Theory — the Fisher-Tippett Theorem, GEV Distributions & the POT Method</h2>
<p>Extreme Value Theory (EVT) is the branch of statistics that deals with the extreme (maximum or minimum) deviations from the median of probability distributions. Unlike classical statistics which focuses on the sample mean and central limit theorem, EVT focuses on the tails — the rare, large deviations that govern risk in finance, hydrology, insurance, and climate science. The central result is the <strong>Fisher-Tippett-Gnedenko theorem</strong> (1928, 1943): the limiting distribution of the block maximum M<sub>n</sub> = max(X<sub>1</sub>, ..., X<sub>n</sub>) of i.i.d. random variables, when appropriately normalized, converges to the <strong>Generalized Extreme Value (GEV) distribution</strong>. The GEV unifies three families: <strong>Gumbel</strong> (ξ = 0, light tails), <strong>Fréchet</strong> (ξ &gt; 0, heavy tails), and <strong>Weibull</strong> (ξ &lt; 0, bounded tails). The <strong>peaks-over-threshold (POT)</strong> method models exceedances over a high threshold using the <strong>Generalized Pareto Distribution (GPD)</strong>, justified by the <strong>Pickands-Balkema-de Haan theorem</strong>. The <strong>extremal index</strong> θ quantifies clustering in stationary sequences. Applications include <strong>Value-at-Risk (VaR)</strong> and <strong>expected shortfall</strong> in finance, the <strong>100-year flood</strong> in hydrology, and extreme weather claims in insurance.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Extreme values are rare by definition — statistics must bridge the gap."</div><div class="attribution">— Emil Julius Gumbel</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📏 The Fisher-Tippett Theorem and the GEV Distribution</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Block Maxima</div><div class="story-title">Fisher-Tippett-Gnedenko Theorem and the Unified GEV</div><div class="story-text">Let X<sub>1</sub>, ..., X<sub>n</sub> be i.i.d. with common distribution F. The <strong>block maximum</strong> is M<sub>n</sub> = max(X<sub>1</sub>, ..., X<sub>n</sub>). The <strong>Fisher-Tippett theorem</strong> (1928), later completed by Gnedenko (1943), states: if there exist sequences a<sub>n</sub> &gt; 0 and b<sub>n</sub> ∈ ℝ such that (M<sub>n</sub> − b<sub>n</sub>)/a<sub>n</sub> converges in distribution to a non-degenerate H, then H belongs to one of three families. The <strong>Gumbel</strong> family: H(z) = exp(−e<sup>−z</sup>) for z ∈ ℝ (ξ = 0), corresponding to light-tailed distributions like the normal and exponential. The <strong>Fréchet</strong> family: H(z) = 0 for z ≤ 0, H(z) = exp(−z<sup>−α</sup>) for z &gt; 0 with α &gt; 0 (ξ = 1/α &gt; 0), corresponding to heavy-tailed distributions like the Student-t and Pareto. The <strong>Weibull</strong> family: H(z) = exp(−(−z)<sup>α</sup>) for z &lt; 0, H(z) = 1 for z ≥ 0 with α &gt; 0 (ξ = −1/α &lt; 0), corresponding to bounded-tail distributions like the uniform. The <strong>unified GEV distribution</strong> combines these three families into a single parametric form: G(z) = exp{−[1 + ξ(z − μ)/σ]<sup>−1/ξ</sup>} for 1 + ξ(z − μ)/σ &gt; 0, where μ ∈ ℝ is the location parameter, σ &gt; 0 is the scale parameter, and ξ ∈ ℝ is the shape parameter. The case ξ = 0 is interpreted as the limit: G(z) = exp{−exp(−(z − μ)/σ)}. The support depends on ξ: for ξ &gt; 0 (Fréchet), the distribution is unbounded above with lower bound μ − σ/ξ; for ξ &lt; 0 (Weibull), the distribution has finite upper bound μ − σ/ξ; for ξ = 0 (Gumbel), the distribution is unbounded both ways. <strong>Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE)</strong> is the standard method for estimating (μ, σ, ξ). The log-likelihood for the GEV is ℓ(μ, σ, ξ) = −m log σ − (1 + 1/ξ) Σ log[1 + ξ(z<sub>i</sub> − μ)/σ] − Σ [1 + ξ(z<sub>i</sub> − μ)/σ]<sup>−1/ξ</sup>, where m is the number of blocks. For ξ = 0, the Gumbel log-likelihood is ℓ(μ, σ) = −m log σ − Σ (z<sub>i</sub> − μ)/σ − Σ exp(−(z<sub>i</sub> − μ)/σ). The <strong>profile likelihood</strong> for individual parameters or return levels is used to construct confidence intervals by inverting the likelihood ratio test. Alternative estimation methods include the <strong>Pickands estimator</strong> (based on sample quantiles) and the <strong>probability weighted moments (PWM)</strong> estimator, which have good small-sample properties.</div><div class="story-moral">The Fisher-Tippett-Gnedenko theorem is the fundamental limit theorem for maxima — it plays the same role for extremes that the central limit theorem plays for sums.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🌍 The Peaks-Over-Threshold Method and the GPD</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">POT Method</div><div class="story-title">Pickands-Balkema-de Haan Theorem and the Generalized Pareto Distribution</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>peaks-over-threshold (POT)</strong> method models the exceedances over a high threshold u. Let X be a random variable with distribution F, and define the <strong>excess</strong> Y = X − u conditional on X &gt; u. The conditional excess distribution function is F<sub>u</sub>(y) = P(X − u ≤ y | X &gt; u) = (F(u + y) − F(u))/(1 − F(u)) for y &gt; 0. The <strong>Pickands-Balkema-de Haan theorem</strong> (1974–75) states: for a sufficiently high threshold u, the distribution F<sub>u</sub>(y) is well approximated by the <strong>Generalized Pareto Distribution (GPD)</strong>: H(y) = 1 − (1 + ξ·y/σ̃)<sup>−1/ξ</sup> for y &gt; 0, where the support is 0 &lt; y &lt; ∞ for ξ ≥ 0 and 0 &lt; y &lt; −σ̃/ξ for ξ &lt; 0. The scale parameter σ̃ = σ + ξ(u − μ) depends on the threshold u, where (μ, σ, ξ) are the GEV parameters of the block maxima. Crucially, the <strong>shape parameter ξ</strong> is exactly the same as the GEV shape parameter — it determines the tail heaviness and is invariant to the threshold. The <strong>threshold selection</strong> is a critical practical issue. The <strong>mean residual life plot</strong> (also called the mean excess function) plots e(u) = E[X − u | X &gt; u] against u. If the GPD model is valid above some u<sub>0</sub>, the mean excess function should be approximately linear in u: e(u) = σ̃/(1 − ξ) + ξ·u/(1 − ξ) for ξ &lt; 1. In practice, one looks for the lowest threshold above which the plot becomes approximately linear. The <strong>parameter estimation</strong> for the GPD is typically done via MLE. The log-likelihood for a sample of n<sub>u</sub> exceedances y<sub>1</sub>, ..., y<sub>n<sub>u</sub></sub> is ℓ(σ̃, ξ) = −n<sub>u</sub> log σ̃ − (1 + 1/ξ) Σ log(1 + ξ·y<sub>i</sub>/σ̃) for ξ ≠ 0, and ℓ(σ̃) = −n<sub>u</sub> log σ̃ − Σ y<sub>i</sub>/σ̃ for ξ = 0. The <strong>MLE properties</strong> depend on ξ: for ξ &gt; −½, the MLE is regular with standard asymptotic normality (√n-consistent, asymptotically normal); for −1 &lt; ξ &lt; −½, the MLE exists but is non-standard with slower convergence rates; for ξ &lt; −1, the MLE does not exist (the likelihood can be made arbitrarily large). The <strong>method of probability-weighted moments</strong> provides an alternative, especially when sample sizes are small.</div><div class="story-moral">The POT method uses data more efficiently than block maxima by including all exceedances over a threshold, but threshold selection requires careful judgment.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 Return Levels and Extremal Dependence</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Return Levels</div><div class="story-title">N-Year Return Level and the Extremal Index</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>N-year return level</strong> z<sub>N</sub> is the level that is exceeded on average once every N years. For the block maxima approach with annual blocks, the return level satisfies P(M<sub>n</sub> &gt; z<sub>N</sub>) = 1/N where n is the block size (typically one year). Inverting the GEV distribution gives: z<sub>N</sub> = μ − σ/ξ (1 − (−log(1 − 1/N))<sup>−ξ</sup>) for ξ ≠ 0, and z<sub>N</sub> = μ − σ log(−log(1 − 1/N)) for ξ = 0. For the POT/GPD approach, the N-year return level is: z<sub>N</sub> = u + σ̃/ξ ((N·n<sub>u</sub>·ζ<sub>u</sub>)<sup>ξ</sup> − 1) for ξ ≠ 0, and z<sub>N</sub> = u + σ̃ log(N·n<sub>u</sub>·ζ<sub>u</sub>) for ξ = 0, where n<sub>u</sub> is the number of exceedances per year and ζ<sub>u</sub> = P(X &gt; u) is estimated by the empirical proportion of exceedances. The <strong>profile likelihood</strong> for return levels provides confidence intervals by profiling out the other parameters. For stationary dependent sequences, the <strong>extremal index</strong> θ (Leadbetter, 1983) quantifies the degree of clustering of extremes. For a stationary sequence {X<sub>i</sub>} satisfying mild mixing conditions, P(M<sub>n</sub> ≤ z) ≈ F(z)<sup>nθ</sup>, where 0 &lt; θ ≤ 1. When θ = 1, extremes occur independently (no clustering); when θ &lt; 1, extremes tend to cluster, with the mean cluster size being 1/θ. The <strong>runs estimator</strong> for θ: count clusters as blocks where the process exceeds u, with a cluster ending after r consecutive observations below u. The estimator is θ̂ = (number of clusters)/(number of exceedances). The <strong>blocks estimator</strong> divides the data into k blocks of length b and estimates θ̂ = (1/k) Σ I(max in block &gt; u) / (1/n) Σ I(X<sub>i</sub> &gt; u). <strong>Declustering</strong> identifies independent clusters by requiring a minimum separation distance r between exceedances — observations within distance r of a preceding exceedance are considered part of the same cluster. The <strong>inter-exceedance time plot</strong> examines the distribution of times between consecutive exceedances to determine an appropriate r. The <strong>block bootstrap</strong> provides non-parametric confidence intervals by resampling blocks of data (preserving dependence structure) and re-estimating parameters.</div><div class="story-moral">Return levels connect probability models to engineering design criteria; the extremal index is essential for correctly modeling extremes in dependent time series.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔥 Applications in Finance and Environmental Science</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Applications</div><div class="story-title">Value-at-Risk, Expected Shortfall, GARCH-EVT, and Spatial Extremes</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Value-at-Risk (VaR)</strong> is a fundamental risk measure in finance. For a loss L (negative return on a portfolio), VaR<sub>α</sub> = inf{x: P(L &gt; x) ≤ 1 − α} at confidence level α (typically 0.95, 0.99, or 0.999). EVT provides tail-consistent estimates of VaR beyond the sample range by fitting a GPD to losses exceeding a high threshold. The <strong>expected shortfall (ES)</strong> (also called CVaR) is ES<sub>α</sub> = E[L | L &gt; VaR<sub>α</sub>], which captures the expected loss given that VaR is exceeded — a coherent risk measure. The <strong>heavy-tailed nature</strong> of financial returns places them in the Fréchet domain: the tail index ξ for stock returns is typically around 0.3 to 0.5. The <strong>GARCH-EVT hybrid</strong> combines volatility modeling (GARCH) for the daily dynamics with EVT on the standardized residuals for tail estimation. First, fit a GARCH(1,1) model r<sub>t</sub> = μ + σ<sub>t</sub>ε<sub>t</sub> with σ<sub>t</sub><sup>2</sup> = ω + αr<sub>t−1</sub><sup>2</sup> + βσ<sub>t−1</sub><sup>2</sup>; second, extract the standardized residuals ε̂<sub>t</sub> and fit a GPD to their upper tail; third, combine to produce conditional VaR and ES forecasts. In <strong>environmental science</strong>, the 100-year flood level is estimated using annual maximum river flow data via the block maxima method — the GEV is fitted to annual maxima, and z<sub>100</sub> is the return level. For <strong>rainfall extremes</strong>, <strong>spatial EVT</strong> models the joint behavior of extremes across locations using <strong>max-stable processes</strong>. The <strong>Smith model</strong> (Gaussian storm profile), the <strong>Schlather model</strong>, and the <strong>Brown-Resnick model</strong> are the most common max-stable processes. Parameter estimation uses <strong>composite likelihood</strong> (pairwise likelihood) because the full likelihood is intractable for most max-stable models. <strong>Bayesian EVT</strong> uses prior distributions for the GEV/GPD parameters — typically weakly informative priors for μ and σ, and a prior for ξ that reflects tail behavior (e.g., a normal prior centered at 0 with variance reflecting uncertainty about tail heaviness). Posterior estimation is done via MCMC (Metropolis-Hastings or Hamiltonian Monte Carlo). <strong>Non-stationary EVT</strong> models the parameters as functions of covariates: μ(t) = β<sub>0</sub> + β<sub>1</sub>·t (linear trend), σ(t) = exp(γ<sub>0</sub> + γ<sub>1</sub>·t) (log-link for positive scale), and ξ(t) as a constant or slowly varying function. Spline models and generalized additive models (GAMs) are used for flexible covariate effects. In <strong>insurance</strong>, extreme weather claims are modeled using EVT to set premiums for catastrophic events and to calculate solvency capital requirements.</div><div class="story-moral">EVT provides the mathematical foundation for quantifying tail risk across disciplines — from financial risk management to climate adaptation and insurance pricing.</div></div>
